CRC-16 Data Integrity Engine with Fault-Injection🛑 What is this?Data gets corrupted over wires, radio links, and storage mediums all the time. A flipped bit doesn't change the message length, meaning a receiver has zero clue the data is garbage unless it checks it.  This project is a complete, hardware-level Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC) communication link. Instead of just doing the math on paper, this implements a full sender, a receiver, and a deliberate fault injector to wreck the data in transit and prove the error detection actually works.  🧱 The Hardware Blockscrc16_core.vhd (The Calculator): The brains of the operation. It processes one byte per clock cycle using the CRC-16/CCITT-FALSE standard. It doesn't know anything about frames or messages; it just crunches the running checksum.  crc16_tx.vhd (The Sender): A Moore state machine. It streams the message "HELLO" one byte per clock cycle, calculates the CRC simultaneously, and tacks the 2-byte checksum (0x49D6) to the very end.  crc16_rx.vhd (The Receiver): Swallows the whole 7-byte frame. Because modulo-2 math is magic, a clean frame run through the engine will always leave a final residue of 0x0000. If the residue is anything else, the data is trashed and it raises an error flag.  crc16_top.vhd (Channel & Fault Injector): Hooks the TX and RX together. Since we can't easily simulate random electrical noise, this block uses an XOR gate to flip specific bits mid-transit so you can actually test the error detection.  
